<Base
  title="Methodology"
  description="How Meyppi classifies delivery: what each verdict means, which measurements drive classification, the evidence fields delivered, and how any finding can be re-checked."
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <span class="kicker">Methodology</span>
      <h1>What the measurements mean, and what we claim.</h1>
      <p class="page-head__sub">
        This page describes what Meyppi measures, how each verdict follows
        from the evidence, and how you can re-check any finding. Calibration
        parameters and scoring thresholds stay private; everything that
        appears in a report is defined below.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow prose">

      <h2>The six verdicts</h2>
      <p>
        Every expected placement receives exactly one verdict. The
        vocabulary is fixed across every report, export and page on this
        site.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Detected — Full.</strong> The creative aired in the published episode and the evidence covers its complete contracted runtime.</li>
        <li><strong>Detected — Partial.</strong> The creative aired, but a measurable part of its runtime is missing (for example, a trimmed tail).</li>
        <li><strong>Version Mismatch.</strong> A version of the creative aired, but the evidence attributes it to a different contracted version than the one expected for that placement.</li>
        <li><strong>Not Detected.</strong> No matching evidence found in the published episode.</li>
        <li><strong>Insufficient Signal.</strong> The published media's quality is too low, or the evidence too sparse, for a defensible result either way.</li>
        <li><strong>Manual Review Required.</strong> The evidence is ambiguous between explanations (for example, additive noise versus a cut); an analyst examines it and the report states what was found.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How a verdict is reached</h2>
      <p>
        Creatives and published episodes are fingerprinted: each file is
        reduced to a constellation of spectral landmark pairs that survives
        compression, loudness processing and background noise. A true
        occurrence of a creative inside an episode appears as thousands of
        landmark pairs agreeing on one consistent time offset; interference,
        cross-talk and music beds do not produce that signature.
      </p>
      <p>
        From the agreed matches, the engine reconstructs the creative's
        timeline inside the episode and measures four things: how much of
        the creative's runtime the matches cover, whether the start or the
        end is missing and by how much, whether there are gaps in the
        middle, and, when several versions of a creative exist, which
        version's evidence outranks the others. Structural findings are
        judged against the creative's own self-match profile, so a
        naturally quiet creative is never mistaken for a partially
        delivered one.
      </p>
      <p>
        Verdicts follow from these measurements by fixed policy, calibrated
        once on labelled corpora. Ambiguous evidence routes to Manual
        Review Required rather than to an unsupported accusation, and the
        report states the reason in each such case.
      </p>

      <h2>Evidence fields</h2>
      <p>
        Every placement's verdict ships with the measurements behind it.
        The same fields appear in the PDF report, the CSV/JSON exports and
        the raw evidence file.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="mono">offset_sec</td><td>Where the match starts inside the published episode.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">covered_time_sec</td><td>How much of the creative's runtime the matches cover.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">coverage_ratio</td><td>Coverage relative to the creative's own self-match profile.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">missing_head_sec / missing_tail_sec</td><td>Missing runtime at the start or end of the creative, in excess of the self-match profile.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">largest_interior_gap_sec</td><td>The largest uncovered stretch inside the matched span.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">match_count</td><td>Number of aligned landmark pairs supporting the match.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">confidence</td><td>Policy confidence for the verdict, from 0 to 1. It is not a probability of legal admissibility.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">evidence_tier</td><td>How the media was obtained: Tier 1 independently captured, Tier 2 independently retrieved from the publisher's public distribution, Tier 3 customer-supplied, Tier 4 unverified. Claim strength in the report follows the tier.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Re-checking a finding</h2>
      <p>
        Audits are reproducible: the same creative files and the same
        published media produce the same report. Every media file entering
        an audit is hashed and its source recorded, so any placement's
        evidence can be traced to a specific file from a specific source
        and re-processed. Raw verification events, the per-match records
        behind every verdict, are exported alongside the report and are
        never discarded after classification.
      </p>

      <h2>Known limits</h2>
      <p>
        The engine's calibration is validated on labelled synthetic
        corpora; real-world performance is being established with each
        engagement. Verification requires the published media to be
        independently observable: dynamically inserted placements that vary
        per listener often cannot be verified from a public feed, and
        host-read segments are outside current scope. Findings are
        algorithmic evidence, not legal proof.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

